From 14481bfa27ba89c21c5f53cc401385a41798e425 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "Claude-claude.ai-sandbox" <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 09:32:50 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(memory): treat user search text as literal FTS5 content
 (DEBT-042)

Base: main f13bfdefcdd197ed1f966de0b1f93037a4168af4. Scope: FTS5 query construction only; DEBT-042's status is intentionally not touched here.

Problem (reproduced before the fix, real SQLite FTS5 3.45.1): SQLiteStorageBackend._fts_escape quoted only tokens containing one of  " ' ( ) * ^ : - +.
Ordinary punctuation (. , ? / % = & $ @ ;) reached MATCH raw and raised "fts5: syntax error" -- 9 of 14 representative queries failed, and
UnifiedMemory.search only logged "L1 FTS5 search failed (non-blocking)", so the FTS5 leg silently contributed nothing. Embedded double quotes were not
doubled, and bare uppercase AND/OR/NOT/NEAR acted as FTS5 operators.

Fix: every whitespace-separated token becomes one double-quoted FTS5 string with embedded quotes doubled; NUL and lone surrogates (which break the FTS5
parser / the sqlite3 binding and cannot occur in indexed text) are removed. The expression is still an SQL bound parameter; search_text, the schema, the
triggers, ranking, filters, the empty-query contract and UnifiedMemory (incl. its existing non-blocking failure path and the vector leg) are unchanged.

Semantics, documented in the docstring: tokens stay implicitly AND-ed; plain words match as before; a token that the tokenizer splits (file.txt, C++,
state-of-the-art) matches as an adjacent phrase (as tokens already quoted for - : ' always did); punctuation-only tokens contribute no terms and a query
with no terms matches nothing; empty/whitespace-only queries keep the established unfiltered contract. ONE intentional semantic change: AND/OR/NOT/NEAR are
now literal terms, not operators.

Tests: 61 new (tests/test_debt042_fts5_literal_queries.py) at three layers -- _fts_escape (exact outputs + a 2000-input fuzz against a real FTS5 table),
SQLiteStorageBackend.search_text (assert which entries come back: operators, column filters, prefix, caret, negation, parentheses, quotes, Unicode, CJK,
emoji, empty / punctuation-only, NUL / surrogate, 1000-token queries, SQL-injection payloads, filters/ordering/deprecation, a genuine DB failure still
raises) and UnifiedMemory.search (FTS leg no longer fails, vector leg still receives the raw query, existing non-blocking failure path unchanged).
37 of the 61 fail on the unmodified code (9 of the original 14 queries). One existing test is rewritten: test_fts5_question_mark_gap_is_pre_existing_and_
unrelated asserted only that '?' was inside the escaped string, which holds for a broken and a fixed escaper alike; it now asserts the corrected behaviour
against a real FTS5 table (renamed ..._is_a_literal_term_since_debt042).
